My answer:

```html
<div style="
  width: 100vw;
  height: 100vh;
  display: flex;
  justify-content: center;
  align-items: center;
">
  <img src="img/world.png">
</div>
```

`justify-content` and `align-items` center the direct children horizontally and vertically. If you intended the parent to be `100vh` wide, keep that value; `100vw` is typically used when you want it to span the viewport width.

Both assistants give valid parent-only centering approaches. Assistant A’s flexbox solution is correct, but its `div` selector would apply those styles to every `div` on the page; a rule targeting just this parent would avoid that. Assistant B provides the same correct flexbox solution and a valid CSS Grid alternative, with examples that apply styles directly to the parent. Its answer is more thorough than necessary, but remains relevant. Neither answer notes that `width: 100vh` may be unintended if the goal is to fill the viewport horizontally.

My final verdict is: [[B>A]]

